Q: EF Core scaffold - unique index with WHERE condition produces a 1:1 mapping instead of 1:n A: <p>First, <a href="https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/issues/11298" rel="nofollow noreferrer">this is a known bug</a>. The Reverse Engineer tooling in <a href="https://github.com/ErikEJ/EFCorePowerTools/wiki/Reverse-Engineering" rel="nofollow noreferrer">EF Core Power Tools</a> can handle this by ignoring the index using the <code>excludedIndexes</code> option. This will generate a collection but <em>not</em> validate how many items satisfy the filter.</p>
<p>Second, there's no DB First in EF Core. The scaffolder does only what the name says - generate (scaffold) classes from an existing database <em>as a convenience</em>. The docs use the terms <code>Reverse Engineer</code> and <code>scaffolding</code> on purpose. You'll have to make changes to the model one way or another. The Object and Relational models are <em>very</em> different and there's no way to magically map one to another. Nor is this desirable - each model has its own desirable features. You can <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ef/core/managing-schemas/scaffolding/templates?tabs=dotnet-core-cli" rel="nofollow noreferrer">use custom code generation templates</a> to change how code is created but at some point, you'll change the generated entities and DbContexts.</p>
<p>That said, there's no easy way to map this pattern.</p>
<ul>
<li>Having a <code>List&lt;TargetRequest&gt;</code> property doesn't guarantee that only one of them will have <code>TargetRequest.IsActive</code> set. How do you ensure the entity is valid when calling <code>SaveChanges</code>? You may have to implement <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.componentmodel.dataannotations.ivalidatableobject?view=net-10.0" rel="nofollow noreferrer">IValidateObject</a>, but EF Core <em><a href="https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/issues/3680" rel="nofollow noreferrer">doesn't</a></em><a href="https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/issues/3680" rel="nofollow noreferrer"> perform validation</a> when saving. The app will have to validate the entities.</li>
<li><em>When</em> do you validate this? When modifying the <code>List&lt;TargetRequest&gt;</code>? Or only when saving? Validating on every change may make editing a <em>lot</em> harder - you'd have to always deactivate the current request before activating another. Imagine binding to a radio button group in a UI. The app could throw depending on the order of UI events.</li>
<li>How do you access the Active request? Perhaps an <code>ActiveRequest=&gt;TargetRequests.Single(t-&gt;IsActive);</code> property would do the job, and complain if the status is wrong.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you decide what to generate, you can customize the generated code using <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ef/core/managing-schemas/scaffolding/templates?tabs=dotnet-core-cli" rel="nofollow noreferrer">custom scaffolding templates</a> or EF Core Power Tools.</p>
<p>Or you could modify the entities by hand, eg using <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/advanced-topics/interface-implementation/mixins-with-default-interface-methods" rel="nofollow noreferrer">default interface methods as mixins</a> to define an <code>IUniqueActive&lt;T&gt;</code> interface that can be applied to just the entities you want? Perhaps something like this :</p>
<pre><code>public interface IHasIsActive
{
    bool IsActive {get;set;}
}

public interface IUniqueActive&lt;TItem&gt; where TItem:IHasActive
{
    public List&lt;TItem&gt; Request {get;}

    public TItem ActiveRequest =&gt; Request.Single(it=&gt;it.IsActive);
}
...

public partial class TargetRequest:IHasIsActive
{
    public bool IsActive {get;set;}
}
public partial class Target:IUniqueActive&lt;TargetRequest&gt;
{

    public List&lt;TargetRequest&gt; Requests {get;set;}
}
</code></pre>
